  "_comment": "🧩️ This subset's own contribution: the mutation vocabulary belongs to ONE subset of ONE standard of ONE artifact, so its (non-)oracle registration and catalog live here rather than in the shared ✏️s/🔌️plugins/🗄️stdio/🔣️oracle.json manifest. Line splitting, line-ending policy and trailing-newline handling are exactly what this subset ITSELF defines — no third-party crate is authoritative over 'is this a text file's line structure' the way lopdf is authoritative over PDF or png is authoritative over PNG, so no oracle is registered here (see noOracleDecisions below).",
  "oracles": [
    {
      "id": "bstr-txt-utf-8-mutate-reader",
      "kind": "third-party-library",
      "ecosystem": "rust",
      "package": "bstr",
      "version": "1.13.1",
      "engine": {
        "family": "bstr",
        "implementation": "bstr UTF-8 byte-string reader",
        "version": "1.13.1"
      },
      "capabilities": [
        "txt-utf-8-mutate"
      ],
      "license": "MIT OR Apache-2.0",
      "testOnly": true,
      "productionReachable": false,
      "networkDuringExecution": false,
      "homepage": "https://github.com/BurntSushi/bstr",
      "rationale": "📖️ The third-party READER the mutate case's oracle role reads every document with: `bstr::ByteSlice::lines_with_terminator` returns each line with the terminator it actually carried, so line boundaries, the LF/CRLF style and the presence of a final terminator are bstr's reading, not this repository's (`../🦀️.rs` `bstr_split`, which refuses a CRLF document that also carries a bare LF: this format reads that LF as line content, bstr reads it as a boundary). The oracle writes the mutated lines back with the subset's documented join and the case compares the result byte for byte (`exact-bytes-v1`). The in-crate re-derivation (`independent_split`) and the `csv` record reader stay as supplements; neither can discharge the requirement alone. bstr 1.13.1, MIT OR Apache-2.0.",
      "comparisonProfiles": [
        "utf8-text-v1",
        "exact-bytes-v1"
      ]
    }
  ],
